---
/** The privacy policy the GDPR requires. */
import { REPO_URL } from '@/core/site';
import { href } from '@/shared/lib/href';
import { CNIL, HOST, MAIL_RELAY, MAIL_STORE, PUBLISHER } from '../../data/facts';
import LegalDocument from '../LegalDocument.astro';

const mail = `mailto:${PUBLISHER.email}`;
---

<LegalDocument title="Privacy policy" subtitle="Politique de confidentialité" updated="2026-11-04">
  <p>
    <strong>In short:</strong> Homeport has no accounts, no analytics, no advertising and no
    telemetry, and this site sets no cookies. The only personal data involved is what any web
    request reveals to the host (your IP address and browser details), and whatever you choose to
    send by email. This policy explains each case, as the General Data Protection Regulation (EU
    2016/679, GDPR) and the French <em>loi Informatique et Libertés</em> require.
  </p>

  <h2 id="controller">Data controller</h2>
  <p>
    {PUBLISHER.name}, publisher of Homeport, is the controller of the processing described here. To
    reach the publisher: <a href={mail}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>. No data protection officer has been
    appointed, as none is required for a processing of this size.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">What is processed, why, and on what basis</h2>

  <h3 id="visits">Visiting this website</h3>
  <p>
    The site is hosted by {HOST.name} ({HOST.service}). When your browser requests a page,{' '}
    {HOST.name} receives, like any web server, your <strong>IP address</strong>, the{' '}
    <strong>page requested</strong>, the <strong>date and time</strong>, and the{' '}
    <strong>browser and system details</strong>
    your browser sends (its user agent). GitHub logs IP addresses to keep its service secure and
    working.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Purpose:</strong> delivering the pages and protecting the site against abuse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interest (GDPR, art. 6(1)(f)) in running a website
      that works and stays secure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention:</strong> set by GitHub, under its{' '}
      <a href={HOST.privacy}>privacy statement</a>. The publisher has no access to these logs and
      receives no visitor statistics.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nothing else is collected. There is no contact form, no newsletter, no comments, no analytics
    and no advertising. Fonts and images are served from this site itself, and search runs in your
    browser from an index on this site, so your searches go nowhere. The few items the site keeps in
    your browser (your theme, for example) never leave it: see the{' '}
    <a href={href('/cookies/')}>cookie policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="downloads">Downloading Homeport</h3>
  <p>
    Installers and packages are downloaded from GitHub Releases and from this site. The same
    technical data as above reaches {HOST.name}, for the same purpose, on the same basis. The
    download page guesses your operating system to suggest the right file; it does so in your
    browser and sends nothing.
  </p>

  <h3 id="app">Using the Homeport app</h3>
  <p>
    Homeport stores your Hosts, keys, certificates, Stacks and registry logins{' '}
    <strong>only on your computer</strong>; none of it is sent to the publisher or anyone else. See{' '}
    <a href={href('/docs/reference/security/')}>Security and your data</a> for exactly what it keeps
    and how secrets are encrypted. The app has no account, telemetry or crash reporting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The one request it makes on its own is the <strong>update check</strong>: at launch and every 6
    hours it downloads a small file from this site listing the latest version. The request carries
    nothing about you, your Hosts or your containers, but it reaches {HOST.name} from your IP
    address, as any download does.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Purpose:</strong> telling you when a new version, including security fixes, is out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interest (GDPR, art. 6(1)(f)) in keeping the software
      you use up to date and secure.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your choice:</strong> turn it off at any time in <strong>Settings → Updates</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To tell you when a newer version of an image you use is published, each Host's Docker Engine
    asks that image's registry (Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry or your own) which version its
    tag points to, when the containers, images or Stacks pages load (each answer is kept for 6
    hours) and when you choose Check for newer images. When you browse Docker Hub tags in the pull
    dialog, your computer asks Docker Hub directly. These requests go to the registries you use,
    never to the publisher, and are handled by each registry under its own privacy policy; with a
    local Host they come from your IP address.
  </p>

  <h3 id="email">Writing to the publisher</h3>
  <p>
    If you email <a href={mail}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>, your <strong>email address</strong>, your
    <strong>name</strong> if you give it, and the <strong>content of your message</strong> are used
    to answer you.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Purpose:</strong> replying to your question or request.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interest (GDPR, art. 6(1)(f)) in answering the people
      who write in; for a request about your rights, the legal obligation to answer it (art.
      6(1)(c)).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention:</strong> up to 3 years after the last exchange, then deleted. A request
      about your rights is kept for the time needed to prove it was handled.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="github">Issues, discussions and contributions</h3>
  <p>
    The issue tracker and the source code are on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>. What you post there
    (your GitHub username, your messages, your commits) is public and handled by GitHub under its
    own terms and <a href={HOST.privacy}>privacy statement</a>. A contribution that has been merged
    stays in the project’s history, which is published under the MIT licence; the publisher can
    remove or edit issues and comments in the repository on request.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recipients">Recipients and processors</h2>
  <p>
    Your data is never sold, rented or shared for marketing. Apart from the publisher, it is handled
    only by:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>{HOST.name}</strong> ({HOST.address}), which hosts the website, the update file, the
      source code and the releases.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>{MAIL_RELAY.name}</strong>, {MAIL_RELAY.country}, which receives mail for the contact
      address and forwards it without keeping a copy (
      <a href={MAIL_RELAY.privacy}>privacy policy</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>{MAIL_STORE.name}</strong> ({MAIL_STORE.address}), whose {MAIL_STORE.service} service
      stores the forwarded mail (<a href={MAIL_STORE.privacy}>privacy policy</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>It may also be disclosed to an authority that is legally entitled to request it.</p>

  <h2 id="transfers">Transfers outside the European Union</h2>
  <p>
    {HOST.name} is based in the United States, so the technical data of a visit, a download or an
    update check is transferred there. GitHub, Inc. is certified under the EU–U.S. Data Privacy
    Framework, which the European Commission found to provide an adequate level of protection
    (adequacy decision of 10 July 2023), and also relies on the Commission’s standard contractual
    clauses. Details are in GitHub’s <a href={HOST.privacy}>privacy statement</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Emails go through {MAIL_RELAY.name} and are stored by {MAIL_STORE.name}, both established in the
    European Union. Google may store and process them on servers outside the European Union,
    including in the United States; its parent company, Google LLC, is certified under the same Data
    Privacy Framework and also relies on standard contractual clauses. Details are in Google’s{' '}
    <a href={MAIL_STORE.privacy}>privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR and the <em>loi Informatique et Libertés</em>, you have the right to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>access</strong> the data held about you and get a copy of it (art. 15);
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>rectify</strong> it if it is inaccurate or incomplete (art. 16);
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>erase</strong> it (art. 17);
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>restrict</strong> its processing (art. 18);
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>object</strong> at any time to processing based on legitimate interest, on grounds
      relating to your situation (art. 21);
    </li>
    <li>
      receive the data you provided in a structured, machine-readable format and have it sent to
      someone else (<strong>portability</strong>, art. 20), where the processing is automated and
      based on your consent or a contract;
    </li>
    <li>
      give <strong>instructions about what happens to your data after your death</strong> (
      <em>loi Informatique et Libertés</em>, art. 85).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>No decision about you is made by automated means, and no profile of you is built.</p>

  <h3 id="exercise">How to exercise them</h3>
  <p>
    Write to <a href={mail}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>, saying which right you want to exercise. You will
    get an answer within one month; this can be extended by two months for a complex request, and
    you would be told why. Proof of identity is asked for only if there is a reasonable doubt about
    who is making the request.
  </p>
  <p>
    The publisher holds no copy of GitHub’s logs. For data GitHub holds about you, including those
    logs and anything linked to your GitHub account, contact GitHub as described in its{' '}
    <a href={HOST.privacy}>privacy statement</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="complaint">Complaining to the CNIL</h3>
  <p>
    If you think your rights have not been respected, you can lodge a complaint with the {CNIL.name}
    ,{CNIL.address}, or online at{' '}
    <a href={CNIL.complaints}>{CNIL.complaints.replace('https://', '')}</a>. You can also complain
    to the data protection authority of the EU country where you live or work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
  <p>
    The site is served only over HTTPS, and nothing it stores in your browser is sent anywhere. The
    app encrypts the secrets it stores and keeps the key in your operating system’s keychain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    This policy changes when what is processed changes. The date at the top shows the latest
    version, and every earlier version is in the{' '}
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/commits/main/website/src/pages/privacy.astro`}>project’s history</a>.
  </p>
</LegalDocument>
